1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">Galaxy kinematics modeling is the computational
bottleneck when astronomers try to jointly</font>

2
00:00:06,104 --> 00:00:10,631
<font color="#FFFFFF">model gravitational lensing and kinematics to
measure the Hubble constant.</font>

3
00:00:10,711 --> 00:00:14,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces SKiNN, the Stellar Kinematics
Neural Network,</font>

4
00:00:14,883 --> 00:00:21,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">a proof-of-concept emulator that reproduces slow
physics-based kinematics calculations at a fraction of the cost.</font>

5
00:00:21,919 --> 00:00:23,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">After a one-time training phase,</font>

6
00:00:23,911 --> 00:00:29,311
<font color="#FFFFFF">SKiNN generates velocity dispersion images that are
accurate to within about one percent</font>

7
00:00:29,391 --> 00:00:31,739
<font color="#FFFFFF">in the scientifically important region,</font>

8
00:00:31,819 --> 00:00:36,471
<font color="#FFFFFF">while running two to three orders of magnitude
faster than existing methods.</font>

9
00:00:36,551 --> 00:00:43,196
<font color="#FFFFFF">This speedup finally makes it feasible to model
spatially resolved kinematic data jointly with lensing data,</font>

10
00:00:43,276 --> 00:00:47,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">addressing the largest source of uncertainty in
Hubble constant measurements.</font>

11
00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:54,095
<font color="#FFFFFF">Gravitational lensing measures the Hubble constant
by comparing time delays between multiple</font>

12
00:00:54,175 --> 00:00:58,133
<font color="#FFFFFF">images of a distant source to a model of the lens
galaxy's mass.</font>

13
00:00:58,213 --> 00:01:04,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">But lensing degeneracies allow many different mass
distributions to reproduce the same image,</font>

14
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:07,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">so independent kinematic constraints are needed to
break them.</font>

15
00:01:07,992 --> 00:01:09,237
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trouble is speed.</font>

16
00:01:09,317 --> 00:01:13,275
<font color="#FFFFFF">Modeling galaxy kinematics with a physics code like
JAM is slow,</font>

17
00:01:13,355 --> 00:01:20,089
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it must be recomputed for every single
likelihood evaluation inside a Markov Chain Monte Carlo sampling.</font>

18
00:01:20,169 --> 00:01:26,714
<font color="#FFFFFF">This makes jointly exploring the full lensing and
kinematics parameter space computationally prohibitive,</font>

19
00:01:26,794 --> 00:01:31,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">forcing modelers to cut corners by fitting the two
components separately.</font>

20
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:35,663
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recently,</font>

21
00:01:35,743 --> 00:01:39,705
<font color="#FFFFFF">spatially resolved kinematics of lensing galaxies
became available through instruments</font>

22
00:01:39,785 --> 00:01:43,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">like the James Webb Space Telescope.</font>

23
00:01:43,828 --> 00:01:47,791
<font color="#FFFFFF">Traditional spherical Jeans models are too
simplistic for this richer data,</font>

24
00:01:47,871 --> 00:01:51,834
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they lack self-consistency with the elliptical
mass models used in lensing.</font>

25
00:01:51,914 --> 00:01:55,876
<font color="#FFFFFF">The natural next step is axisymmetric modeling with
software such as JAM,</font>

26
00:01:55,956 --> 00:01:59,919
<font color="#FFFFFF">but JAM is expensive.</font>

27
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:03,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">Existing frameworks that combine it with lens
modeling either fit the two separately or</font>

28
00:02:04,042 --> 00:02:08,005
<font color="#FFFFFF">remain far too slow for full joint parameter exploration.</font>

29
00:02:08,085 --> 00:02:12,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight of this work is that JAM's slow
physics can be emulated by a neural network,</font>

30
00:02:12,127 --> 00:02:16,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">keeping the physics of the overall model while
removing the computational bottleneck.</font>

31
00:02:16,470 --> 00:02:20,988
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core contribution is SKiNN, the Stellar
Kinematics Neural Network.</font>

32
00:02:21,068 --> 00:02:25,586
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is the first time the strategy of replacing an
expensive model component with a neural</font>

33
00:02:25,666 --> 00:02:30,183
<font color="#FFFFFF">network has been applied to the kinematic-modeling
aspect of gravitational lens modeling.</font>

34
00:02:30,263 --> 00:02:34,781
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

35
00:02:34,861 --> 00:02:39,379
<font color="#FFFFFF">SKiNN emulates only the JAM physics calculation
rather than being applied directly to observations.</font>

36
00:02:39,459 --> 00:02:43,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">This design choice keeps the physics of the overall
model intact while exploiting the</font>

37
00:02:44,057 --> 00:02:48,574
<font color="#FFFFFF">speed and versatility of neural networks.</font>

38
00:02:48,654 --> 00:02:53,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a roughly 300-fold speedup at
sub-percent accuracy,</font>

39
00:02:53,252 --> 00:02:57,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">which finally makes joint lensing plus kinematics
modeling computationally feasible.</font>

40
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:02,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">SKiNN precalculates JAM for use in lensing applications.</font>

41
00:03:02,872 --> 00:03:07,514
<font color="#FFFFFF">Formally,</font>

42
00:03:07,594 --> 00:03:12,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is a function that maps an eight-dimensional
vector of galaxy parameters into a d by d image of vrms,</font>

43
00:03:12,315 --> 00:03:16,957
<font color="#FFFFFF">the quadratic sum of velocity dispersion and
rotational velocity.</font>

44
00:03:17,037 --> 00:03:21,679
<font color="#FFFFFF">The eight inputs describe a power-law elliptical
mass distribution and an elliptical Sérsic light profile,</font>

45
00:03:21,759 --> 00:03:26,401
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus the galaxy inclination and orbital anisotropy.</font>

46
00:03:26,481 --> 00:03:31,123
<font color="#FFFFFF">The architecture consists of five blocks,</font>

47
00:03:31,203 --> 00:03:35,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">each with two two-dimensional convolutional layers
followed by an upsampling layer and a ReLU nonlinearity,</font>

48
00:03:35,925 --> 00:03:40,566
<font color="#FFFFFF">for about seven million trainable parameters.</font>

49
00:03:40,646 --> 00:03:45,288
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is trained by minimizing a standard
mean-squared-error loss with the Adam optimizer,</font>

50
00:03:45,368 --> 00:03:50,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">using a dataset generated by JAM through the GLEE
software so the network learns to mimic the JAM procedure.</font>

51
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:53,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">The training set is constructed entirely with JAM,</font>

52
00:03:54,048 --> 00:03:57,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">the physics code SKiNN is meant to imitate.</font>

53
00:03:57,194 --> 00:04:00,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors created five thousand input-output pairs,</font>

54
00:04:01,072 --> 00:04:05,528
<font color="#FFFFFF">using four thousand for training, five hundred for
validation,</font>

55
00:04:05,608 --> 00:04:08,309
<font color="#FFFFFF">and five hundred held out for testing.</font>

56
00:04:08,389 --> 00:04:11,309
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each velocity image is 551 by 551 pixels,</font>

57
00:04:11,389 --> 00:04:14,674
<font color="#FFFFFF">generated at higher resolution than real data.</font>

58
00:04:14,754 --> 00:04:19,137
<font color="#FFFFFF">Creating a single image with JAM takes about
fifteen seconds,</font>

59
00:04:19,217 --> 00:04:22,503
<font color="#FFFFFF">which underscores why emulation is worthwhile.</font>

60
00:04:22,583 --> 00:04:29,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">The network is implemented in PyTorch with PyTorch
Lightning and trained on five Tesla P100 GPUs,</font>

61
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:33,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">each with sixteen gigabytes of memory, over roughly
one day.</font>

62
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:39,152
<font color="#FFFFFF">Once trained,</font>

63
00:04:39,232 --> 00:04:44,014
<font color="#FFFFFF">SKiNN produces a velocity image from an input
parameter vector in about fifty milliseconds on a single GPU,</font>

64
00:04:44,094 --> 00:04:48,876
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is roughly three hundred times faster than JAM.</font>

65
00:04:48,956 --> 00:04:53,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">Accuracy is assessed on the five hundred held-out
test images by computing the relative error at each pixel.</font>

66
00:04:53,818 --> 00:04:58,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">While some outer regions can show errors of a few percent,</font>

67
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:03,462
<font color="#FFFFFF">the important innermost region, where real data
actually constrains the model,</font>

68
00:05:03,542 --> 00:05:08,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">is matched to within plus or minus one percent for
almost all pixels.</font>

69
00:05:08,404 --> 00:05:13,186
<font color="#FFFFFF">Averaged over entire images,</font>

70
00:05:13,266 --> 00:05:18,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">the median absolute error is about 0.47 percent and
the ninetieth percentile is about 1.1 percent.</font>

71
00:05:18,128 --> 00:05:22,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both are comfortably below the typical
two-percent-or-greater systematic uncertainty in real velocity measurements.</font>

72
00:05:23,290 --> 00:05:26,869
<font color="#FFFFFF">As a proof-of-concept workshop paper,</font>

73
00:05:26,949 --> 00:05:30,528
<font color="#FFFFFF">this work does not present a formal ablation study.</font>

74
00:05:30,608 --> 00:05:34,187
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead,</font>

75
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:37,846
<font color="#FFFFFF">performance is characterized by the distribution of
per-pixel errors across the five hundred test images,</font>

76
00:05:37,926 --> 00:05:41,504
<font color="#FFFFFF">and by comparing inference on GPU versus CPU,</font>

77
00:05:41,584 --> 00:05:45,163
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the GPU is about ten times faster.</font>

78
00:05:45,243 --> 00:05:48,822
<font color="#FFFFFF">The analysis also distinguishes the innermost
two-arcsecond region,</font>

79
00:05:48,902 --> 00:05:52,481
<font color="#FFFFFF">where errors stay within one percent, from the
outer parts of the image,</font>

80
00:05:52,561 --> 00:05:56,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">where errors can rise to a few percent but the data
provides little constraint anyway.</font>

81
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:58,516
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here are the headline numbers.</font>

82
00:05:58,596 --> 00:06:02,184
<font color="#FFFFFF">SKiNN runs about three hundred times faster than JAM,</font>

83
00:06:02,264 --> 00:06:08,759
<font color="#FFFFFF">taking roughly fifty milliseconds per image on a
GPU compared to about fifteen seconds for JAM.</font>

84
00:06:08,839 --> 00:06:13,811
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its error is under one percent within the innermost
two-arcsecond region,</font>

85
00:06:13,891 --> 00:06:19,901
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a median absolute error around 0.47 percent
and a ninetieth percentile of about 1.1</font>

86
00:06:19,981 --> 00:06:22,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">percent across five hundred test images.</font>

87
00:06:22,750 --> 00:06:28,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">The network maps an eight-dimensional parameter
vector to a velocity image and has about</font>

88
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:31,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">seven million trainable parameters.</font>

89
00:06:31,262 --> 00:06:33,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">It was trained on five thousand samples,</font>

90
00:06:34,031 --> 00:06:39,626
<font color="#FFFFFF">split four thousand for training and five hundred
each for validation and testing,</font>

91
00:06:39,706 --> 00:06:42,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">over roughly one day on five Tesla P100 GPUs.</font>

92
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:47,077
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

93
00:06:47,157 --> 00:06:51,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">By training a neural network to emulate the slow
kinematics physics of JAM,</font>

94
00:06:51,194 --> 00:06:55,151
<font color="#FFFFFF">SKiNN achieves roughly a three-hundred-fold speedup
at sub-percent accuracy in the region that matters.</font>

95
00:06:55,231 --> 00:06:59,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">This removes the main computational bottleneck and
makes it feasible to jointly model</font>

96
00:06:59,268 --> 00:07:03,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">gravitational lensing together with spatially
resolved kinematics,</font>

97
00:07:03,305 --> 00:07:07,262
<font color="#FFFFFF">which corrects for the largest source of
uncertainty in measuring the Hubble constant.</font>

98
00:07:07,342 --> 00:07:11,299
<font color="#FFFFFF">More broadly,</font>

99
00:07:11,379 --> 00:07:15,336
<font color="#FFFFFF">the strategy of swapping an expensive but
calculable model piece for a trained neural network,</font>

100
00:07:15,416 --> 00:07:19,373
<font color="#FFFFFF">while keeping the surrounding physics intact,</font>

101
00:07:19,453 --> 00:07:23,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">likely transfers to many other scientific modeling
problems.</font>
